\section{A Model with Decisions in Both Directions}

Scheduling determines when a temporary occupies storage; the
location selected for that temporary can limit which schedules
are feasible. Casta\~neda Lozano and Schulte's \emph{Survey on
Combinatorial Register Allocation and Instruction Scheduling}
distinguishes integer programming, constraint programming, and
partitioned Boolean quadratic optimization among other approaches.
A joint model might give each operation an issue cycle $s_i$,
an activation bit $b_i$, and a choice of instruction form. A
selected form fixes its operand classes, result latencies, and
resource reservations. For an active definition and its selected
uses, occupancy extends from the required destination reservation
point through the last read, measured in ordered subpositions when
needed. Values assigned overlapping hardware units must have
disjoint occupancy. Argument and live-out locations are boundary
constraints, not preferences the solver can override.

For scheduling alone, Wilken, Liu, and Heffernan's \emph{Optimal
Instruction Scheduling Using Integer Programming} uses
optimality-preserving graph transformations before the integer
program. In a simple time-indexed formulation, $x_{it}=1$ means
operation $i$ issues at $t$, so $\sum_t x_{it}=b_i$. A reservation
of unit $u$ at offsets 0 and 1 contributes
$x_{it}+x_{i,t-1}$ to the load on $u$ at time $t$. Sum such
terms across all operations and bound them by the capacity of $u$.
Precedence for a selected def--use pair imposes
$s_j\ge s_i+\ell_{ij}$; optional forms need conditional constraints
so an inactive operation neither consumes resources nor guarantees
that a use is defined. Graph simplifications proved for a fixed
schedule model need new justification when they remove a choice
that matters to register occupancy.

\section{A Four-Cycle Witness}

Consider an artificial target with two issue slots, one memory unit
and one output unit. Two independent outputs consume $x$ and $y$
from immutable, nonfaulting memory. Normally each read issues a
load with two-cycle result latency, then a one-cycle output
instruction reads the temporary. There is only one register
available for the temporaries; each load reserves it at issue
until the end of the output instruction's issue cycle. Pure
scheduling would issue loads at 0 and 1 and outputs at 2 and 3,
but the intervals overlap. With the unchanged forms and one
register, issue the first load at 0, its output at 2, the second
load at 3, and the second output at 5. Completion at 6 meets the
lower bound for serializing the two register occupancies.

Suppose the $y$ output also has a fused memory form that reserves
both units only in its issue cycle, completes three cycles later,
and takes no temporary register. Select that form at 1, the $x$
load at 0, and the $x$ output at 2. Completion is at 4 with one
register. Completion by 3 would require both a cycle-0 fused
instruction and a cycle-0 $x$ load, which would conflict on the
memory unit. Thus the four-cycle witness is optimal \emph{in this
model}. If the fused form held the output unit for two cycles,
the proposed witness would fail its resource check at cycle 2.

\section{Bounded Search and Independent Checks}

In \emph{Combinatorial Register Allocation and Instruction
Scheduling}, Casta\~neda Lozano, Carlsson, Hjort Blindell, and
Schulte use optional copies in Unison to represent transformations
including splitting, spilling, and coalescing. The use of a
copied temporary, its operation's activation, and its selected
home must agree. Allowing an unselected copy to provide a value
would produce a fast but fictitious schedule. For an engineering
deployment, seed search with a checked heuristic solution, keep
the best feasible incumbent, and distinguish timeout with a
validated improvement from proof of optimality. If the objective
is integer cycles and bounds are 8 and 10 at timeout, an
incumbent of 10 gives a gap of at most two cycles under the model,
not knowledge of the actual processor's performance.
Test the solver with the four-cycle witness, then disable the
fused form and require the six-cycle optimum. This catches a
missing activation constraint even if both returned schedules
look reasonable in a cycle dump.

The dossier's \emph{Compiler as a Constraint Solver} describes how
a bounded region could expose such decisions to a solver while
retaining an independent validation boundary. Verify chosen forms,
all copies, and boundary locations after emission as in
Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-240}. Reserve exact search for cases where
measured improvement or value as a heuristic oracle pays the
compilation cost.

