\section{Prune with a Relaxation the Model Actually Implies}

A search node fixes some forms, issue cycles and register choices.
To bound its possible completion time, discard contention and retain
only dependencies guaranteed across its remaining choices. A compulsory
operation's minimum latency is taken across its still-legal forms.
Likewise, each retained edge must have a valid minimum distance,
even if the source is not yet fixed to one form. Use a known feasible
incumbent under the same objective and model; if the solver also
optimizes code size at equal makespan, the test below cannot prune ties.

\begin{sudocode}[A dependence-only lower bound]
\State $L\gets 0$
\ForAll{compulsory operations $i$ in dependency order}
  \State $t[i]\gets$ fixed issue time of $i$, or 0 if unfixed
  \ForAll{retained incoming edges $(j,i)$}
    \State $t[i]\gets\max(t[i],t[j]+\ell_{\min}(j,i))$
  \EndFor
  \State $L\gets\max(L,t[i]+latency_{\min}[i])$
    \Comment{Ignore contention, not compulsory work}
\EndFor
\If{$L\geq$ incumbent makespan}
  \State prune \Comment{No strict improvement is possible}
\EndIf
\end{sudocode}

Suppose a search branch fixes a copy to issue at cycle 2, with
latency one. Its result feeds a compulsory operation with a
three-cycle form or a two-cycle form. The consumer cannot issue
before 3, so the bound is 5. With a validated incumbent of 5 the
branch is pruned; with an incumbent of 6 it is not, even if other
resource conflicts later prove that 5 is impossible. If a solver
mistakenly uses the three-cycle latency in the bound, it can wrongly
discard a feasible five-cycle improvement. This makes a tiny
two-form problem a useful fixture for the pruning implementation.

Every actual feasible issue time is at least $t[i]$ by induction on
the retained dependency order; inconsistent fixed times cannot
introduce a better solution. The pass is linear in the retained
nodes and edges. In particular, omit a copy that could be deactivated
rather than bounding the entire branch by a partly selected spill
plan. A weaker bound may cost search time; a stronger but invalid one
can make a false claim of optimality.
